\chapter{The MuLL command line and system installation}\label{ch:mull-cli}
\begin{MuObjectives}
\item Choose the C++ or D implementation without changing a source program.
\item Inspect a compilation stage without executing user code.
\item Distinguish package discovery from native library linking.
\end{MuObjectives}

The \api{mull} command is the shared interface to the two MuDSL hosts. Its
default implementation is \api{MuDSL.hpp}; \api{--engine=d} selects
\api{MuDSL.d}. Each compiled worker performs its own parsing, MuLLIER analysis,
Mu3AC lowering, and code emission. The D implementation does not call the C++
compiler worker, and an unavailable implementation is an error rather than an
implicit fallback.

\section{Build once, install under a prefix}
From the repository root, the standalone entry point is:
\begin{code}
cmake -S mudsl/mull -B build/mull -DMULL_ENABLE_MULIB=OFF
cmake --build build/mull --parallel 4
ctest --test-dir build/mull --output-on-failure
cmake --install build/mull --prefix "$HOME/.local"
\end{code}
The normal \api{mudsl/CMakeLists.txt} entry point builds the same CLI targets.
The core-only switch above omits the optional MuLIB bridge, not the five native
base packages or thirty portable packages. Enable that bridge when its native
headers and runtime sources are available. A D compiler is optional for the
C++ host; LDC or DMD also builds the D native runtime archive. A GDC-only build
currently provides the D interpreter/compiler worker, not that native archive.

CMake installs the command under \api{bin}, private workers/configuration under
\api{libexec/mull}, native runtime archives under the configured library
directory, and package data under \api{share/mull/stdlib}. The command's
configuration is prefix-relative, including staged installations. Add a local
prefix's binary and man directories to the appropriate shell search paths.
\api{mull --list-engines} reports the installed workers.

\section{Execution and arguments}
\begin{code}
mull -p -e 'return 6*7;'
mull --engine=d -p example.mull -- input.txt -option
mull --backend=x86-64 -p example.mull
\end{code}
The interpreter remains the default backend. Without an emission option, the
native backend builds and runs a temporary executable. \api{-p} prints the
final value; otherwise only explicit program output is printed. A returned
number is a language value, not a process exit status.

The immutable CLI global \api{Args} contains arguments following the separate
\api{--}, without the source or executable name. Input \api{-} reads standard
input; \api{-e} supplies an expression/program directly. With a terminal and no
source, or with \api{--repl}, the interpreter retains bindings between complete
one-line programs. \api{:quit} ends that session.

\section{Stopping the pipeline}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}lX@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Option} & \textbf{Artifact or action} \\
\midrule
\api{--check} & Parse and analyze, with no evaluation or artifact. \\
\api{--emit=ast} & Original parsed tree in the MuLLIER interchange format. \\
\api{--emit=mullier} & Analyzed, optionally optimized MuLLIER. \\
\api{--emit=mu3ac} & Versioned per-function three-address code. \\
\api{-S} & GNU assembler source for Linux x86-64. \\
\api{-c} & An ELF relocatable object. \\
\api{--emit=exe} & A native executable linked with the selected host runtime. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
The corresponding \api{--stop-after} names are \api{parse}, \api{mullier},
\api{mu3ac}, \api{assembly}, \api{object}, and \api{link}. Text artifacts default
to standard output; objects default to \api{a.o}, executables to \api{a.out}.
Use \api{-o} to name an artifact and \api{--run} to run an explicitly emitted
executable after linking. Mu3AC output is currently an inspection format, not
a second source-input language for the command.

\begin{MuCaution}[Checking is not execution]
Compile-only stages do not evaluate the entry program or preload packages.
They therefore cannot establish that every late-bound name, dynamic type,
package import, or foreign symbol will succeed at execution/link time.
\end{MuCaution}

\section{Two distinct library paths}
\api{-I DIR} adds a MuLL discovery root. Explicit roots precede nonempty
\api{MULL_PATH} entries and the installed standard-library root. Discovery
looks for \api{DIR/name/Package.mull}; it does not load a shared object.
\api{--no-default-paths} keeps only explicit roots. \api{--no-stdlib} omits
native package registration, not the core \api{G} record. \api{--load NAME}
preloads a package at execution time and reports import failures immediately.
Native artifacts snapshot these paths and preloads; package files are not
embedded into the executable.

By contrast, \api{-L DIR}, \api{-l NAME}, and \api{--link-object FILE} are native
linker inputs. A symbol becomes a MuLL callable only with an explicit ABI
binding. For example, a C library can supply:
\begin{cppcode}
/* In a C translation unit, or extern "C" when compiled as C++. */
double custom_square(double value) { return value * value; }
\end{cppcode}
\begin{code}
mull --backend=x86-64 -L ./lib -lcustom \
  --extern=Square=custom_square:1 -p -e 'return Square(7);'
\end{code}
The narrow foreign ABI accepts zero through eight \api{double} arguments and
returns one \api{double}. It checks MuLL arity, numeric arguments, and finite
results, but cannot verify a linked function's actual C signature. Shared
libraries may additionally need \api{--rpath DIR} or platform loader setup.
The richer typed MuLIB wrapper is a separate provider, not this ABI.

\section{Operational controls and manuals}
\api{-O0} disables transformations in both IRs, never semantic analysis;
\api{-O1} enables bounded optimization. Custom rule inputs are
\api{--mullier-rules DIR} and \api{--mu3ac-rules FILE}. Execution budgets include
\api{--max-steps}, \api{--max-call-depth}, \api{--max-frames},
\api{--max-source-bytes}, and the native-only \api{--max-values} arena limit.
\api{--cc}, \api{--cxx}, and \api{--dc} choose tool executables; \api{--verbose}
prints their argument vectors. The driver never constructs shell commands.
Trusted compiler options and native libraries are nevertheless executable
authority, not a security boundary.

The installed \api{mull(1)} is the complete CLI reference. The single
\api{mull(5)} manual describes the five native base packages, thirty portable
packages, optional MuLIB provider, discovery, value contracts, and costs.
Chapters 46--70 and 73 remain the narrative language/library introduction;
the manpages are the concise system-facing reference.

\begin{MuSummary}
\item Host selection and backend selection are independent decisions.
\item A stopping stage emits only what that stage establishes.
\item Package roots and native linker search paths are not interchangeable.
\end{MuSummary}
